<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hakka people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hakka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hakka are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> people whose ancestors migrated in waves from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to the hill country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>, keeping a distinct Hakka language. Many emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, where they worked as miners and farmers, notably in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan">Hong Xiuquan</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>, was Hakka, and Hakka communities supplied many of its first followers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hakka-people.jpg" alt="Hakka people" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KotaKinabalu_Sabah_Gaya-Street-Sunday-Market-17.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
